<emu-clause id="formal-host-debugger-source">
  <h1>The Debugging Facility and Native Function Source Text</h1>
  <p>Host choices of <emu-xref href="#sec-debugger-statement-runtime-semantics-evaluation"></emu-xref> and <emu-xref href="#sec-function.prototype.tostring"></emu-xref>.</p>

  <emu-primitive id="prim-debuggerenabled" name="DebuggerEnabled">
    <emu-signature>DebuggerEnabled(): a Boolean</emu-signature>
    <p>Whether an implementation-defined debugging facility is available and enabled. A host whose answer is *true* runs DebuggerAction, which the invariants of the essential internal methods bound (<emu-xref href="#sec-invariants-of-the-essential-internal-methods"></emu-xref>).</p>
  </emu-primitive>

  <emu-primitive id="prim-debuggeraction" name="DebuggerAction">
    <emu-signature>DebuggerAction(): ~unused~</emu-signature>
    <p>An implementation-defined debugging action. An implementation must not allow the invariants of the essential internal methods to be circumvented in any manner (<emu-xref href="#sec-invariants-of-the-essential-internal-methods"></emu-xref>), and the model reads this as: every object stays an object and keeps its internal slots but [[Extensible]] and [[Prototype]]; its [[Extensible]] only turns from *true* to *false*; an object whose [[Extensible]] is not *true* keeps its [[Prototype]] and gains no property; and a non-configurable property stays, its attributes changing only as the invariants of [[GetOwnProperty]] allow.</p>
  </emu-primitive>

  <emu-primitive id="prim-nativefunctionsource" name="NativeFunctionSource">
    <emu-signature>NativeFunctionSource(_func_: a function object): a String</emu-signature>
    <p>An implementation-defined String source code representation of _func_. The representation must have the syntax of a |NativeFunction|. If _func_ has an [[InitialName]] internal slot, _func_.[[InitialName]] is a String, and _func_.[[InitialName]] is matched by |NativeFunctionAccessor?| |PropertyName|, the portion of the returned String that would be matched by |NativeFunctionAccessor?| |PropertyName| must be _func_.[[InitialName]]; a String is so matched when it is *"get"* or *"set"* (the portion is the name whether it is read as the |NativeFunctionAccessor| or as the |PropertyName|), or when the |NativeFunction| that follows `function ` with it has it as that portion. If _func_.[[InitialName]] is the empty String, the returned String has neither a |NativeFunctionAccessor| nor a |PropertyName|, so that its portion is empty (<emu-xref href="#erratum-NativeFunctionInitialName"></emu-xref>). The returned String is the string-concatenation of *"function "*, a name, and *"() { [native code] }"*, where the name is the empty String or, after *"get "*, *"set "* or nothing, an ASCII |IdentifierName| without escapes or *"[Symbol."*, one, and *"]"*; its portion matched by |NativeFunctionAccessor?| |PropertyName| is the name. This is narrower than the syntax of a |NativeFunction|, which also admits white space, line terminators and comments between its tokens and non-ASCII or escaped names: a host whose representation is one of those does not meet this law, and an [[InitialName]] that is matched but is not such a name has no String this law admits (a choice of the model, not a reading of the text: decisions.md, <code>nativefunctionsource-form</code>).</p>
  </emu-primitive>

  <emu-reading clause="sec-debugger-statement-runtime-semantics-evaluation" steps="1 1.a" hashes="64de63531870e0c3f62bb278fea9aa12fac8c9d5408ec8751c4348e52e37e351 5a953d6a8b05efc5a84e90125ddaa285edae7ef9b3f8e77b4aeb77b96cba4f76">
    <emu-alg reading="definition">
      1. If DebuggerEnabled() is *true*, then
        1. Perform DebuggerAction().
        1. Return a new implementation-defined Completion Record.
    </emu-alg>
  </emu-reading>

  <emu-reading clause="sec-function.prototype.tostring" steps="3" hashes="e123217b503fa9ffe925e358f91a9ccb4e2394cf9feab1bd9d0062564ce105d7">
    <emu-alg reading="definition">
      1. If _func_ is a built-in function object, then
        1. If _func_ has an [[InitialName]] internal slot and _func_.[[InitialName]] is a String, then
          1. Let _name_ be _func_.[[InitialName]].
          1. Let _matched_ be *false*.
          1. If _name_ is *"get"* or _name_ is *"set"*, then
            1. Set _matched_ to *true*.
          1. Else if _name_ is not the empty String, then
            1. Let _native_ be ParseGoal(StringToCodePoints(the string-concatenation of *"function "*, _name_, and *"() { [native code] }"*), |NativeFunction|, *false*, *false*, *false*).
            1. Let _closed_ be ParseGoal(StringToCodePoints(the string-concatenation of *"function "*, _name_, the code unit 0x000A (LINE FEED), and *"() { [native code] }"*), |NativeFunction|, *false*, *false*, *false*).
            1. If _native_ is not ~empty~, _closed_ is not ~empty~, and _native_ contains a |PropertyName| Parse Node, then
              1. Let _key_ be CodePointsToString(the source text matched by the first |PropertyName| Parse Node contained within _native_).
              1. If _native_ does not contain a |NativeFunctionAccessor| Parse Node, then
                1. If _name_ is _key_, set _matched_ to *true*.
              1. Else,
                1. Let _accessor_ be CodePointsToString(the source text matched by the first |NativeFunctionAccessor| Parse Node contained within _native_).
                1. Let _n_ be the length of _name_.
                1. If the substring of _name_ from 0 to the length of _accessor_ is _accessor_ and the substring of _name_ from _n_ - the length of _key_ to _n_ is _key_, set _matched_ to *true*.
          1. If _name_ is not the empty String and _matched_ is *false*, return an implementation-defined String source code representation of _func_. The representation must have the syntax of a |NativeFunction|. Additionally, if _func_ has an [[InitialName]] internal slot and _func_.[[InitialName]] is a String, the portion of the returned String that would be matched by |NativeFunctionAccessor?| |PropertyName| must be _func_.[[InitialName]].
        1. Return NativeFunctionSource(_func_).
    </emu-alg>
    <emu-note>
      <p>_matched_ is whether _name_ is matched by |NativeFunctionAccessor?| |PropertyName| with nothing before or after it: _name_ is *"get"* or *"set"* (the |NativeFunction| is ambiguous, and either way its portion is _name_), or the |NativeFunction| that follows `function ` with _name_ parses and its portion matched by |NativeFunctionAccessor?| |PropertyName| is all of _name_. That portion starts at _name_'s start exactly when _name_ starts with its accessor (or is its key), and ends at _name_'s end exactly when _name_ ends with its key and _closed_ parses: _native_'s key can end _name_ without ending its portion only where a `//` comment in _name_ hides the appended *"() { [native code] }"*, and then _closed_, whose line feed ends the comment, does not parse. Where _name_ is matched, or is the empty String (a |NativeFunction| without a |NativeFunctionAccessor| or a |PropertyName|), NativeFunctionSource's law is the step's requirement, narrowed to its form (decisions.md, <code>nativefunctionsource-form</code>). Otherwise no String meets the requirement, and the pinned text is left as it is (<emu-xref href="#erratum-NativeFunctionInitialName"></emu-xref>).</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-reading>

  <emu-reading clause="sec-function.prototype.tostring" steps="4" hashes="515690cd6fc93ef5a3003fdfc96de8479ec080b17d17046e19a25303eca8c29d">
    <emu-alg reading="definition">
      1. If _func_ is an Object and IsCallable(_func_) is *true*, return NativeFunctionSource(_func_).
    </emu-alg>
    <emu-note>
      <p>The step's requirement that the representation have the syntax of a |NativeFunction| is NativeFunctionSource's law, narrowed to its form (decisions.md, <code>nativefunctionsource-form</code>).</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-reading>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="formal-host-regexp-source">
  <h1>The Source Text of a Regular Expression</h1>
  <p>The host choice of <emu-xref href="#sec-escaperegexppattern"></emu-xref>.</p>

  <emu-primitive id="prim-escapepattern" name="EscapePattern">
    <emu-signature>EscapePattern(_pattern_: a String, _flags_: a String): a String</emu-signature>
    <p>A String in the form of the |Pattern| that _flags_ selects, equivalent to _pattern_ interpreted as UTF-16 encoded Unicode code points, in which certain code points are escaped. The Abstract Closure that would result from evaluating it as that |Pattern| must behave identically to the one _pattern_ gives, and the string-concatenation of *"/"*, it, *"/"*, and _flags_ must be parsable as a |RegularExpressionLiteral| whose body it is. It is a function of _pattern_ and _flags_: multiple calls with the same values produce identical results.</p>
  </emu-primitive>

  <emu-reading clause="sec-escaperegexppattern" steps="4 5" hashes="c71828c3c3c465ff240c2a492e5e2b9ecf8840ef103966046fe271eeea2111c6 f60aefb7834699ed6ed624cb1664b39217741dadc2229762b10de7db19d6db1c">
    <emu-alg reading="definition">
      1. Let _escapedPattern_ be EscapePattern(_pattern_, _flags_).
    </emu-alg>
    <emu-note>
      <p>Step 5 constrains the choice of step 4 and takes no action of its own. The form, the escapes of step 5, the identical behaviour and the identical results for the same _pattern_ and _flags_ are EscapePattern's law; step 5's examples are informative.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-reading>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="formal-host-locale">
  <h1>Locale-Sensitive Methods Without ECMA-402</h1>
  <p>Host choices of the methods whose specification, for an implementation without the ECMA-402 Internationalization API, leaves their locale-sensitive result to the host environment's current locale.</p>

  <emu-primitive id="prim-localeuppercase" name="LocaleUppercase">
    <emu-signature>LocaleUppercase(_cps_: a List of code points): a List of code points</emu-signature>
    <p>A new List of the code points of _cps_ mapped to upper case by the conventions of the host environment's current locale (<emu-xref href="#sec-string.prototype.tolocaleuppercase"></emu-xref>): toUppercase(_cps_), except in the few cases (such as Turkish) where the rules for that language conflict with the regular Unicode case mappings.</p>
  </emu-primitive>

  <emu-primitive id="prim-localelowercase" name="LocaleLowercase">
    <emu-signature>LocaleLowercase(_cps_: a List of code points): a List of code points</emu-signature>
    <p>A new List of the code points of _cps_ mapped to lower case by the conventions of the host environment's current locale (<emu-xref href="#sec-string.prototype.tolocalelowercase"></emu-xref>): toLowercase(_cps_), except in the few cases (such as Turkish) where the rules for that language conflict with the regular Unicode case mappings.</p>
  </emu-primitive>

  <emu-primitive id="prim-localecompare" name="LocaleCompare">
    <emu-signature>LocaleCompare(_string_: a String, _thatValue_: a String): a Number</emu-signature>
    <p>The result, other than *NaN*, of an implementation-defined locale-sensitive comparison of _string_ with _thatValue_ (<emu-xref href="#sec-string.prototype.localecompare"></emu-xref>): negative when _string_ is ordered before _thatValue_, positive when it is ordered after, and zero otherwise, by an ordering of all Strings that every call follows (a consistent comparator defining a total ordering); *+0*<sub>𝔽</sub> when the two are canonically equivalent according to the Unicode Standard. It has no effect on ECMAScript state.</p>
  </emu-primitive>

  <emu-primitive id="prim-localenumberstring" name="LocaleNumberString">
    <emu-signature>LocaleNumberString(_x_: a Number or a BigInt): a String or ~empty~</emu-signature>
    <p>A String that represents _x_ formatted according to the conventions of the host environment's current locale, or ~empty~ where the host returns the same thing as `toString` (<emu-xref href="#sec-number.prototype.tolocalestring"></emu-xref>, <emu-xref href="#sec-bigint.prototype.tolocalestring"></emu-xref>). It has no effect on ECMAScript state.</p>
  </emu-primitive>

  <emu-primitive id="prim-localedatestring" name="LocaleDateString">
    <emu-signature>LocaleDateString(_portion_: a portion (~date~, ~time~ or ~all~), _thisValue_: an ECMAScript language value): a String, ~empty~ or ~throw~</emu-signature>
    <p>A String that represents the “date” portion (~date~), the “time” portion (~time~) or the whole (~all~) of the Date _thisValue_ in the current time zone in a convenient, human-readable form that corresponds to the conventions of the host environment's current locale, or ~empty~ where the host returns the form `toDateString`, `toTimeString` or `toString` returns (<emu-xref href="#sec-date.prototype.tolocaledatestring"></emu-xref>, <emu-xref href="#sec-date.prototype.tolocaletimestring"></emu-xref>, <emu-xref href="#sec-date.prototype.tolocalestring"></emu-xref>). These methods are not generic: their *this* value "must be an object that has a [[DateValue]] internal slot" (<emu-xref href="#sec-properties-of-the-date-prototype-object"></emu-xref>), and the text gives no consequence for any other value, for which the host gives a String or ~throw~ (a *TypeError*, as every algorithmic Date method throws); ~empty~ only for an object with a [[DateValue]] internal slot. It has no effect on ECMAScript state.</p>
  </emu-primitive>

  <emu-clause id="formal-string.prototype.tolocaleuppercase" type="built-in function" behaviour-of="sec-string.prototype.tolocaleuppercase">
    <h1>String.prototype.toLocaleUpperCase ( [ _reserved1_ [ , _reserved2_ ] ] )</h1>
    <p>This method "works exactly the same as `toUpperCase` except that it is intended to yield a locale-sensitive result corresponding with conventions of the host environment's current locale" (<emu-xref href="#sec-string.prototype.tolocaleuppercase"></emu-xref>): <emu-xref href="#formal-string.prototype.touppercase"></emu-xref>'s steps with LocaleUppercase. They read neither optional parameter.</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _thisValue_ be the *this* value.
      1. Perform ? RequireObjectCoercible(_thisValue_).
      1. Let _string_ be ? ToString(_thisValue_).
      1. Let _sText_ be StringToCodePoints(_string_).
      1. Let _upperText_ be LocaleUppercase(_sText_).
      1. Let _uppercaseString_ be CodePointsToString(_upperText_).
      1. Return _uppercaseString_.
    </emu-alg>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="formal-string.prototype.tolocalelowercase" type="built-in function" behaviour-of="sec-string.prototype.tolocalelowercase">
    <h1>String.prototype.toLocaleLowerCase ( [ _reserved1_ [ , _reserved2_ ] ] )</h1>
    <p>This method "works exactly the same as `toLowerCase` except that it is intended to yield a locale-sensitive result corresponding with conventions of the host environment's current locale" (<emu-xref href="#sec-string.prototype.tolocalelowercase"></emu-xref>): <emu-xref href="#sec-string.prototype.tolowercase"></emu-xref>'s steps with LocaleLowercase. They read neither optional parameter.</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _thisValue_ be the *this* value.
      1. Perform ? RequireObjectCoercible(_thisValue_).
      1. Let _string_ be ? ToString(_thisValue_).
      1. Let _sText_ be StringToCodePoints(_string_).
      1. Let _lowerText_ be LocaleLowercase(_sText_).
      1. Let _lowercaseString_ be CodePointsToString(_lowerText_).
      1. Return _lowercaseString_.
    </emu-alg>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-reading clause="sec-string.prototype.localecompare" steps="4" hashes="0bc7eeb6c32daeeeaf9d86911dbc57ffdae22c2236d17d438925628370fbaab0">
    <emu-alg reading="definition">
      1. Let _thatValue_ be ? ToString(_that_).
      1. Return LocaleCompare(_string_, _thatValue_).
    </emu-alg>
    <emu-note>
      <p>The steps prepare the Strings "before performing the comparisons"; the method "returns a Number other than *NaN* representing the result of" the comparison of _string_ with _thatValue_, which is LocaleCompare's. The steps read neither optional parameter.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-reading>

  <emu-clause id="formal-number.prototype.tolocalestring" type="built-in function" behaviour-of="sec-number.prototype.tolocalestring">
    <h1>Number.prototype.toLocaleString ( [ _reserved1_ [ , _reserved2_ ] ] )</h1>
    <p>This method "produces a String value that represents this Number value formatted according to the conventions of the host environment's current locale"; "it is permissible, but not encouraged, for it to return the same thing as `toString`" (<emu-xref href="#sec-number.prototype.tolocalestring"></emu-xref>). "This Number value" is ThisNumberValue of the *this* value (<emu-xref href="#sec-properties-of-the-number-prototype-object"></emu-xref>). The steps read neither optional parameter.</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _x_ be ? ThisNumberValue(*this* value).
      1. Let _s_ be LocaleNumberString(_x_).
      1. If _s_ is ~empty~, return Number::toString(_x_, 10).
      1. Return _s_.
    </emu-alg>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="formal-bigint.prototype.tolocalestring" type="built-in function" behaviour-of="sec-bigint.prototype.tolocalestring">
    <h1>BigInt.prototype.toLocaleString ( [ _reserved1_ [ , _reserved2_ ] ] )</h1>
    <p>This method "produces a String value that represents this BigInt value formatted according to the conventions of the host environment's current locale"; "it is permissible, but not encouraged, for it to return the same thing as `toString`" (<emu-xref href="#sec-bigint.prototype.tolocalestring"></emu-xref>). "This BigInt value" is ThisBigIntValue of the *this* value (<emu-xref href="#sec-properties-of-the-bigint-prototype-object"></emu-xref>). The steps read neither optional parameter.</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _x_ be ? ThisBigIntValue(*this* value).
      1. Let _s_ be LocaleNumberString(_x_).
      1. If _s_ is ~empty~, return BigInt::toString(_x_, 10).
      1. Return _s_.
    </emu-alg>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="formal-date.prototype.tolocaledatestring" type="built-in function" behaviour-of="sec-date.prototype.tolocaledatestring">
    <h1>Date.prototype.toLocaleDateString ( [ _reserved1_ [ , _reserved2_ ] ] )</h1>
    <p>This method "returns a String value. The contents of the String are implementation-defined, but are intended to represent the “date” portion of the Date in the current time zone in a convenient, human-readable form that corresponds to the conventions of the host environment's current locale" (<emu-xref href="#sec-date.prototype.tolocaledatestring"></emu-xref>): LocaleDateString's, or `toDateString`'s form (<emu-xref href="#sec-date.prototype.todatestring"></emu-xref>). The steps read neither optional parameter.</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _dateObj_ be the *this* value.
      1. Let _s_ be LocaleDateString(~date~, _dateObj_).
      1. If _s_ is ~throw~, throw a *TypeError* exception.
      1. If _s_ is ~empty~, then
        1. Perform ? RequireInternalSlot(_dateObj_, [[DateValue]]).
        1. Let _tv_ be _dateObj_.[[DateValue]].
        1. If _tv_ is *NaN*, return *"Invalid Date"*.
        1. Let _t_ be LocalTime(_tv_).
        1. Return DateString(_t_).
      1. Return _s_.
    </emu-alg>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="formal-date.prototype.tolocalestring" type="built-in function" behaviour-of="sec-date.prototype.tolocalestring">
    <h1>Date.prototype.toLocaleString ( [ _reserved1_ [ , _reserved2_ ] ] )</h1>
    <p>This method "returns a String value. The contents of the String are implementation-defined, but are intended to represent the Date in the current time zone in a convenient, human-readable form that corresponds to the conventions of the host environment's current locale" (<emu-xref href="#sec-date.prototype.tolocalestring"></emu-xref>): LocaleDateString's, or `toString`'s form (<emu-xref href="#sec-date.prototype.tostring"></emu-xref>). The steps read neither optional parameter.</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _dateObj_ be the *this* value.
      1. Let _s_ be LocaleDateString(~all~, _dateObj_).
      1. If _s_ is ~throw~, throw a *TypeError* exception.
      1. If _s_ is ~empty~, then
        1. Perform ? RequireInternalSlot(_dateObj_, [[DateValue]]).
        1. Let _tv_ be _dateObj_.[[DateValue]].
        1. Return ToDateString(_tv_).
      1. Return _s_.
    </emu-alg>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="formal-date.prototype.tolocaletimestring" type="built-in function" behaviour-of="sec-date.prototype.tolocaletimestring">
    <h1>Date.prototype.toLocaleTimeString ( [ _reserved1_ [ , _reserved2_ ] ] )</h1>
    <p>This method "returns a String value. The contents of the String are implementation-defined, but are intended to represent the “time” portion of the Date in the current time zone in a convenient, human-readable form that corresponds to the conventions of the host environment's current locale" (<emu-xref href="#sec-date.prototype.tolocaletimestring"></emu-xref>): LocaleDateString's, or `toTimeString`'s form (<emu-xref href="#sec-date.prototype.totimestring"></emu-xref>). The steps read neither optional parameter.</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _dateObj_ be the *this* value.
      1. Let _s_ be LocaleDateString(~time~, _dateObj_).
      1. If _s_ is ~throw~, throw a *TypeError* exception.
      1. If _s_ is ~empty~, then
        1. Perform ? RequireInternalSlot(_dateObj_, [[DateValue]]).
        1. Let _tv_ be _dateObj_.[[DateValue]].
        1. If _tv_ is *NaN*, return *"Invalid Date"*.
        1. Let _localTime_ be LocalTime(_tv_).
        1. Return the string-concatenation of TimeString(_localTime_) and TimeZoneString(_tv_).
      1. Return _s_.
    </emu-alg>
  </emu-clause>
</emu-clause>
